[{:db/id "q-6669/selection/0f0db3a18f1d79c4863315eefe754cc5306e04e7aeebf4414ffedb95d6f1bc97" :answer/id #uuid "c47db36c-d5d4-5ebc-ba45-5f9e0e650fda" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "12" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-6669/selection/a0e0e68f25b8b46aafef3b74aff7beff7fddc6de5bf36921680b04c7b0e6ffaa" :answer/id #uuid "08edba16-cd8f-568b-a56b-be0a8ce5aa95" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-6669/selection/56019ff80d11d678dbd5eca83c729f420f1b6a8ad4259032846618d6b48aa0bc" :answer/id #uuid "5a51eefa-f12c-5bce-ae4b-5b02d4838dbe" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "10" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-6669/selection/7845f5bf94d2cfe99f73682a34529955af46f8f5d6f59bb198a07cbc245060af" :answer/id #uuid "fc1af6fc-5897-5a50-a2fa-425d697dbf48" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "8" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-6669/selection/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" :answer/id #uuid "0984caa8-4320-5ed9-8fdb-1a3869a55695" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-6669/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "0f485854-2381-534f-af7c-b4632b31f533" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-6669/selection/0f0db3a18f1d79c4863315eefe754cc5306e04e7aeebf4414ffedb95d6f1bc97" :answer-field/choices ["q-6669/selection/0f0db3a18f1d79c4863315eefe754cc5306e04e7aeebf4414ffedb95d6f1bc97" "q-6669/selection/a0e0e68f25b8b46aafef3b74aff7beff7fddc6de5bf36921680b04c7b0e6ffaa" "q-6669/selection/56019ff80d11d678dbd5eca83c729f420f1b6a8ad4259032846618d6b48aa0bc" "q-6669/selection/7845f5bf94d2cfe99f73682a34529955af46f8f5d6f59bb198a07cbc245060af" "q-6669/selection/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-6669" :question/id #uuid "d1dcb895-1da8-588d-8542-43eae8980235" :question/math-academy-id "q-6669" :question/problem "For the polynomial $f(x)={x}^{3}-10{x}^{2}+27x-18,$ how many *potential* roots are given by the rational roots theorem?" :question/worked-solution "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial:\n\n- The constant term is $-18,$ and its factors are $\\pm 1,\\,\\pm 2,\\,\\pm 3,\\,\\pm 6,\\,\\pm 9$ and  $\\pm 18.$\n\n- The leading coefficient is $1,$ and its factors are $\\pm 1.$\n\nFirst, we list out all the possibilities for the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\pm \\frac{1}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{2}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{3}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{6}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{9}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{18}{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we reduce the fractions to lowest terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\pm 1, & \\pm 2, & \\pm 3, & \\pm 6, & \\pm 9, & \\pm 18.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that none of these potential roots are duplicates. \n\nThis makes a total of $12$ potential roots. (Remember that each $\\pm $ sign accounts for $2$ roots, and $2\\cdot 6=12.$)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-6669/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1b5f9ee9-a489-5048-8346-0116a19e9b4a"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-6669"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-83840/selection/7845f5bf94d2cfe99f73682a34529955af46f8f5d6f59bb198a07cbc245060af" :answer/id #uuid "d88aee8e-e78a-5122-a231-8c4b90098694" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "8" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-83840/selection/a0e0e68f25b8b46aafef3b74aff7beff7fddc6de5bf36921680b04c7b0e6ffaa" :answer/id #uuid "1e669f11-b7c3-5b46-bb9f-089630405392" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-83840/selection/56019ff80d11d678dbd5eca83c729f420f1b6a8ad4259032846618d6b48aa0bc" :answer/id #uuid "236c8a02-b896-543c-afdd-4f31b8cff76b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "10" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-83840/selection/83c2285763389b0bc8b13e9777da06a48782d28e9c124603d3003cc4dc2e718a" :answer/id #uuid "91d5b2ef-e22d-578c-9c9b-5f87dc0bc77a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "14" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-83840/selection/0f0db3a18f1d79c4863315eefe754cc5306e04e7aeebf4414ffedb95d6f1bc97" :answer/id #uuid "ea18803f-48cc-559b-977d-0d6cf60c5fd4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "12" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-83840/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "bec5345e-a178-50b6-9b58-5aa8b0bece85" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-83840/selection/0f0db3a18f1d79c4863315eefe754cc5306e04e7aeebf4414ffedb95d6f1bc97" :answer-field/choices ["q-83840/selection/7845f5bf94d2cfe99f73682a34529955af46f8f5d6f59bb198a07cbc245060af" "q-83840/selection/a0e0e68f25b8b46aafef3b74aff7beff7fddc6de5bf36921680b04c7b0e6ffaa" "q-83840/selection/56019ff80d11d678dbd5eca83c729f420f1b6a8ad4259032846618d6b48aa0bc" "q-83840/selection/83c2285763389b0bc8b13e9777da06a48782d28e9c124603d3003cc4dc2e718a" "q-83840/selection/0f0db3a18f1d79c4863315eefe754cc5306e04e7aeebf4414ffedb95d6f1bc97"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-83840" :question/id #uuid "da17c4ed-4ab3-57b1-81d4-3a233a0bd886" :question/math-academy-id "q-83840" :question/problem "For the polynomial $f(x)=2{x}^{5}+21{x}^{3}+16x+14,$ how many *potential* roots are given by the rational roots theorem?" :question/worked-solution "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial:\n\n- The constant term is $14,$ and its factors are $\\pm 1,$ $\\pm 2,$ $\\pm 7,$ and $\\pm 14.$\n\n- The leading coefficient is $2,$ and its factors are $\\pm 1,$ $\\pm 2.$\n\nFirst, we list out all the possibilities for the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\pm \\frac{1}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{2}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{2}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{2}{2}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{7}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{7}{2}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{14}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{14}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we reduce the fractions to lowest terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\pm 1, & \\pm \\frac{1}{2}, \\\\ \\pm 2, & \\pm 1, \\\\ \\pm 7, & \\pm \\frac{7}{2}, \\\\ \\pm 14, & \\pm 7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that several potential roots are duplicates (shown above in red). Removing these duplicates, and listing those remaining in order from least to greatest in absolute value, we have\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{1}{2},\\,\\pm 1,\\,\\pm 2,\\,\\pm \\frac{7}{2}\\,\\pm 7,\\,\\pm 14.\n$$\n\nThis makes a total of $12$ potential roots. (Remember that each $\\pm $ sign accounts for $2$ roots, and $2\\cdot 6=12.$)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-83840/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1b5f9ee9-a489-5048-8346-0116a19e9b4a"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-83840"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-112874/selection/19d5466e84349f416e7f242f3eae0e8ce9f2558634cf0796beb62f8e60f5ea80" :answer/id #uuid "d96bfb3c-421e-5de6-abb3-2ede78197b68" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{6}{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-112874/selection/ae9425c9e204310ed598f729a242e0b42a2acbbf125bff30bc2080e10b8cf473" :answer/id #uuid "7d86c78a-bf63-5297-8757-4287e538900a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-112874/selection/067f8394ee42d8135073fd0c196cd07b15b62bf00b668a6dd30a825b7176ad3f" :answer/id #uuid "841a9b67-0c96-5f53-bea1-8160ed0f03f6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{10}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-112874/selection/8bb646af439794acfbd313a6a7eb86a2818f1f792118f7583677724bca2d9903" :answer/id #uuid "4fff97d8-690b-5ee3-ad1c-5fbcb603bfea" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-112874/selection/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" :answer/id #uuid "68f7b0ef-9692-524e-b6a1-eac62613dbb3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-112874/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "19c44a43-bfe1-55b9-83cf-27bb482f8148" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-112874/selection/19d5466e84349f416e7f242f3eae0e8ce9f2558634cf0796beb62f8e60f5ea80" :answer-field/choices ["q-112874/selection/19d5466e84349f416e7f242f3eae0e8ce9f2558634cf0796beb62f8e60f5ea80" "q-112874/selection/ae9425c9e204310ed598f729a242e0b42a2acbbf125bff30bc2080e10b8cf473" "q-112874/selection/067f8394ee42d8135073fd0c196cd07b15b62bf00b668a6dd30a825b7176ad3f" "q-112874/selection/8bb646af439794acfbd313a6a7eb86a2818f1f792118f7583677724bca2d9903" "q-112874/selection/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-112874" :question/id #uuid "c0a9853b-5f08-5935-9f58-b1a4704f7537" :question/math-academy-id "q-112874" :question/problem "According to the rational roots theorem, which of the following **cannot** be a root of the polynomial $f(x)=10{x}^{5}-7{x}^{2}+2x-8?$" :question/worked-solution "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial $f(x),$ the constant term is $-8$ and the leading coefficient is $10.$ So, any rational roots of $f(x)$ must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of }-8}{\\text{factor of }10}.\n$$\n\nOf the given answer choices, only $-\\frac{6}{5}=\\frac{-6}{5}$ does not take the above form. The numerator $(-6)$ is not a factor of $-8.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-112874/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a75b47ec-8d5a-545e-b915-daca92320322"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-112874"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-112875/selection/b13ac151352392d38026eed875b911708c50c896d6c09463410ac041030163ff" :answer/id #uuid "45db3c94-38e5-5ad3-a37c-7f0e88992089" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-112875/selection/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03" :answer/id #uuid "00900e9b-3910-5b72-9916-acb816889f25" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-112875/selection/4e2cb386499e1926f074a7bcd422a63175a237204a3823f0fa6c86b56d301873" :answer/id #uuid "6db0cb1c-ddd2-5d21-93a2-77d908629596" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-112875/selection/e9bc05c525076ace5dedc0dc52c4f8497f441cd3bf97b666736f75acd9994b26" :answer/id #uuid "b9bc64bd-bfeb-5d48-a031-fe0aa87cbfd6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-112875/selection/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" :answer/id #uuid "ed59bdbb-62c4-542b-8280-0eb3578c2cfb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-112875/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "5cfc2450-6b19-591a-9c9e-b5e95faf3ba4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-112875/selection/b13ac151352392d38026eed875b911708c50c896d6c09463410ac041030163ff" :answer-field/choices ["q-112875/selection/b13ac151352392d38026eed875b911708c50c896d6c09463410ac041030163ff" "q-112875/selection/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03" "q-112875/selection/4e2cb386499e1926f074a7bcd422a63175a237204a3823f0fa6c86b56d301873" "q-112875/selection/e9bc05c525076ace5dedc0dc52c4f8497f441cd3bf97b666736f75acd9994b26" "q-112875/selection/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-112875" :question/id #uuid "22d2c056-6e08-5c31-9b27-7a4a15727ffa" :question/math-academy-id "q-112875" :question/problem "According to the rational roots theorem, which of the following **cannot** be a root of the polynomial $f(x)=2{x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}+6x-4?$" :question/worked-solution "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial $f(x),$ the constant term is $-4$ and the leading coefficient is $2.$ So, any rational roots of $f(x)$ must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of }-4}{\\text{factor of }2}.\n$$\n\nOf the given answer choices, only $-3$ does not take the above form. The value $-3$ can be interpreted as a fraction $-\\frac{3}{1},$ and the numerator $(3)$ is not a factor of $-4.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-112875/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a75b47ec-8d5a-545e-b915-daca92320322"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-112875"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-112984/selection/f80535409f78d0e1b5cd0ceb7e72783093e3cde8adf28a5032c83c6dc6b5e490" :answer/id #uuid "7125f12d-70fc-5a1b-aadd-5877cb5712c2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-112984/selection/35f5572ccca3a8c38d3ca06e2a92b51065f48e45d29833e19f4eb153cae096fc" :answer/id #uuid "2ef500b3-9469-509a-9fd1-b05afbd76e7a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-112984/selection/2b998a440c758092569557828849b9a2d05282247ea8a11a5727b6cadcbcb080" :answer/id #uuid "a8f24117-c57e-5e0f-80af-4799fc02e647" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{7}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-112984/selection/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" :answer/id #uuid "d06dbdbd-e5e3-5a84-93be-74fbdeed745f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-112984/selection/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" :answer/id #uuid "128001bd-3ff3-5293-922d-80a141576af8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-112984/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "0341e623-1c81-5888-8332-5f8b086b9f27" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-112984/selection/2b998a440c758092569557828849b9a2d05282247ea8a11a5727b6cadcbcb080" :answer-field/choices ["q-112984/selection/f80535409f78d0e1b5cd0ceb7e72783093e3cde8adf28a5032c83c6dc6b5e490" "q-112984/selection/35f5572ccca3a8c38d3ca06e2a92b51065f48e45d29833e19f4eb153cae096fc" "q-112984/selection/2b998a440c758092569557828849b9a2d05282247ea8a11a5727b6cadcbcb080" "q-112984/selection/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" "q-112984/selection/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-112984" :question/id #uuid "bf13e900-4dce-558a-80db-3ec5cf963787" :question/math-academy-id "q-112984" :question/problem "Given that the polynomial $f(x)=4{x}^{3}-15{x}^{2}+12x+4$ has a root in the interval $(-\\frac{1}{2},0),$ find the sum of the **distinct** real roots of $f(x).$" :question/worked-solution "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial $f(x),$ the constant term is $4$ and the leading coefficient is $4.$ So, any rational roots of $f(x)$ must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of }4}{\\text{factor of }4}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the possible rational roots are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{1}{4},\\,\\pm \\frac{1}{2},\\,\\pm 1,\\,\\pm 2,\\,\\pm 4.\n$$\n\nOf these roots, the only root in the interval $(-\\frac{1}{2},0)$ is $-\\frac{1}{4}.$\n\nUsing the synthetic division, we get the following:\n\n|  | ${x}^{3}$ | ${x}^{2}$ | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-\\frac{1}{4}$ | $4$ | $-15$ | $12$ | $4$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $-1$ | $4$ | $-4$ |\n|  | $4$ | $-16$ | $16$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nf(x)=(x+\\frac{1}{4})(4{x}^{2}-16x+16).\n$$\n\nSince the highest-degree factor is now a quadratic, we can continue factoring using the usual methods:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =(x+\\frac{1}{4})(4{x}^{2}-16x+16) \\\\  & =4(x+\\frac{1}{4})({x}^{2}-4x+4) \\\\  & =(4x+1)(x-2)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the factors equal to $0,$ we find the roots $x=-\\frac{1}{4},2.$ Summing these roots, we get\n\n$$\n-\\frac{1}{4}+2=\\frac{7}{4}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-112984/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "001262b8-82b7-5421-80ac-8608b20d4be1"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-112984"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]}]
